      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Given that $-4{e}^{z}+7=5,$ then $z=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{z}+7 & =5 \\\\ -4{e}^{z} & =-2 \\\\ {e}^{z} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{z} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{z}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$",
